Subject: Fitted values in Random Walk model
Message-ID: <Q9yVQuk8nYE.Fj-BDePpDwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/Q9yVQuk8nYE/m/Fj-BDePpDwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear all,

I have abundance data on 100 bird populations along 20 years and I am trying to evaluate the effect of 3 management scenarios on population dynamics. Therefore, each population is repeated 3 times in my dataset along 20 years each, so I have 6,000 observations.

I have fitted a temporal model with a random walk trend for each management scenario. In addition, I have incorporated the population identifier as random intercept:

Model <- inla(BirdNumber ~ Intercept + Management Scenario + f(Year, Scenario1, model= "rw2") + f(Year, Scenario2, model= "rw2") + f(Year, Scenario3, model= "rw2") + f(Population, model= "iid", family= "nbinomial")

Once the model is fitted I would like to obtain mean fitted values without taking into account the Population random intercept. How can I do that? If I run Model$summary.fitted.values it takes into account the population random intercept and it is not very representative. On the other hand, I have tried to fit them by hand using Model$summary.fixed and Model$summary.random$year but I don't get good estimates for the posterior 95% Bayesian Credible Interval.

Thank you very much for your help

Best regards,
Julia

